---
// The landing (W2.1). Each sentence that says what Wayfarer does names the claim that proves it
// (docs/claims.md): e2e/site.spec.ts holds every ID named here to the ledger.
import Page from "../layouts/Page.astro";
import Claims from "../components/Claims.astro";
import Header from "../components/Header.astro";

const REPO = "https://github.com/AshwinSathian/wayfarer";
---

<Page
  title="Wayfarer: an API client in your browser"
  description="An API client that runs in your browser and keeps your work on your device. No install, no account, no server of its own."
>
  <Header />
  <main>
    <section class="hero">
      <h1>An API client that runs in your browser and keeps your work on your device.</h1>
      <p class="lede">
        Open it and send a request. There is nothing to install and no account to make, and Wayfarer has no server of its own: the request goes
        from your tab to the host you name. <Claims ids={["C-001"]} />
      </p>
      <p class="actions">
        <a class="open" href="/app/">Open Wayfarer</a>
        <a href="/docs/self-hosting/">Host it yourself</a>
      </p>
      <img
        class="shot"
        src="/app.jpg"
        srcset="/app-720.jpg 720w, /app.jpg 1440w"
        sizes="(max-width: 760px) 100vw, 1100px"
        width="1440"
        height="900"
        alt="Wayfarer with a GET request to api.example.com and its JSON response, status 200"
      />
    </section>

    <section>
      <h2>Your work stays on your device</h2>
      <p>
        Collections, environments and history are kept in your browser's IndexedDB. <Claims ids={["C-002"]} /> The values you put in the vault
        are stored only as ciphertext, under a key that is held in memory and gone when you reload. <Claims ids={["C-003", "C-005"]} /> Nothing
        the app stores, exports or copies holds a secret's plaintext. <Claims ids={["C-007"]} />
      </p>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h2>Bring what you already have</h2>
      <p>
        Postman collections and environments, Insomnia exports, OpenAPI and Swagger descriptions, HAR files and cURL commands import, and you see
        what a file will add before anything is stored. <Claims ids={["C-054", "C-056", "C-058", "C-059", "C-060"]} /> Scripts have the parts of
        Postman's <code>pm</code> API that the compatibility document lists, and run in a sandbox that reaches nothing but that API. <Claims ids={["C-040", "C-052"]} /> The scripts of a collection
        you imported run only after you have read them. <Claims ids={["C-051"]} />
      </p>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h2>When the browser is in the way</h2>
      <p>
        Before you send, the composer says what the browser will do to the request: the headers it will not send, and whether it will ask the
        server first. <Claims ids={["C-049"]} /> For a request a browser cannot make, the Local Bridge sends it from your machine. <Claims
          ids={["C-041", "C-064"]}
        />
      </p>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h2>The same results in CI</h2>
      <p>
        <code>npx wayfarer-cli run</code> runs a collection with the app's engine and gives the results the app gives. <Claims ids={["C-065"]} />
      </p>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h2>Every claim here has a test</h2>
      <p>
        The IDs on this page are rows of the <a href="/docs/claims/">claims ledger</a>. Each row names the automated test
        that proves it. CI runs them on every change, those about the page in Chromium, Firefox and WebKit, and fails when a claim has no test.
      </p>
      <p class="actions"><a class="open" href="/app/">Open Wayfarer</a></p>
    </section>
  </main>
  <footer>
    <a href="/changelog/">Changelog</a>
    <a href={`${REPO}/blob/main/SECURITY.md`}>Security</a>
    <a href={`${REPO}/blob/main/LICENSE`}>MIT licence</a>
  </footer>
</Page>
